  "slug": "graph-neural-networks-a7c7",
  "summary": "Recent preprints and papers test predictive gains in drug and peptide modeling against baselines, controlled splits, and stratified evaluation.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is that predictive gains in molecular and biomedical modeling are increasingly tested against controls before being read as biological insight. The olfaction audit compares MoLFormer and ChemBERTa with RDKit and Morgan fingerprints and finds weak model-human alignment and no clear incremental value over simpler representations. AMPBench-MT applies a homology-controlled benchmark across antimicrobial peptide recognition, species-conditioned pMIC regression, and safety endpoints. Other items propose new architectures or representations, including PMRD's zero-shot consensus pharmacological response domain, GRAIN's active-ingredient state-space modeling, and DuSSM's reported 82.27% F1 on DDI-2013. What is new relative to accuracy-led reporting is the expectation that a gain must survive baselines, controlled splits, and stratification.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
